0/1 KNAPSACK PROBLEM (Dynamic Programming)

This is a CORE DP problem — super important for placements, exams, and viva.

1️⃣ Problem Statement

You are given:

n items

Each item has:

a weight wt[i]

a value val[i]

A knapsack with maximum capacity W

🔒 You can take each item at most once (0 or 1 time).

👉 Find the maximum total value that can be put into the knapsack without exceeding capacity.

2️⃣ Why is it called 0/1 Knapsack?

0 → item not chosen

1 → item chosen once

❌ You cannot take fractional items

❌ You cannot take same item multiple times

3️⃣ Real-World Example

🎒 You have a bag with limited capacity and expensive gadgets.
You want to maximize value, not weight.

4️⃣ Approach Used: Dynamic Programming
Why DP?

Overlapping subproblems

Optimal substructure

Recursive solution is exponential ❌

DP reduces it to polynomial time ✅

5️⃣ DP State Definition (VERY IMPORTANT)
dp[i][w] = Maximum value using
           first i items
           with knapsack capacity w

6️⃣ Transition Formula

If weight of current item ≤ capacity:

dp[i][w] = max(
    val[i-1] + dp[i-1][w - wt[i-1]],   // take item
    dp[i-1][w]                         // don't take
)


Else:

dp[i][w] = dp[i-1][w]

7️⃣ Algorithm (Step-by-Step)

Create DP table dp[n+1][W+1]

Initialize first row and column as 0

Loop over items

Loop over capacity

Apply choice logic

Answer will be dp[n][W]

8️⃣ Java Code (Clean & Interview-Ready)
class Knapsack01 {

    public static int knapsack(int[] wt, int[] val, int W) {
        int n = wt.length;

        // DP table
        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][W + 1];

        // Build dp table
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int w = 1; w <= W; w++) {

                // If item can be included
                if (wt[i - 1] <= w) {
                    dp[i][w] = Math.max(
                            val[i - 1] + dp[i - 1][w - wt[i - 1]],
                            dp[i - 1][w]
                    );
                } 
                // Item cannot be included
                else {
                    dp[i][w] = dp[i - 1][w];
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n][W];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] wt = {2, 3, 4};
        int[] val = {4, 5, 6};
        int W = 5;

        System.out.println(
            "Maximum Profit = " + knapsack(wt, val, W)
        );
    }
}

9️⃣ Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 DP Table Creation
int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][W + 1];


Rows → items

Columns → capacity

Extra row & column for base case

🔹 Loop Over Items
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)


Considering items one by one

🔹 Loop Over Capacity
for (int w = 1; w <= W; w++)


Try every capacity from 1 to W

🔹 Choice Logic
Math.max(
  val[i-1] + dp[i-1][w - wt[i-1]],
  dp[i-1][w]
)


Pick OR skip the item

Choose maximum profit

